{{[INPUT]}}
Yes. I would make it a **single scoring-and-selection equation**, rather than treating “richness” and “quality” as separate formulas.

## Destined Information Extraction Equation

$$
\boxed{
D(x)=
\underbrace{
\frac{
R(x)\,Q(x)\,E(x)\,C(x)\,F(x)
}{
1+\lambda\,U(x)
}
}_{\text{Destined Value}}
\;
\underbrace{
\mathbb{1}\!\left[D(x)\ge\theta\right]
}_{\text{extraction gate}}
}
$$

where:

* \(x\) = candidate information/data/state
* \(R(x)\) = **information richness**
* \(Q(x)\) = **quality**
* \(E(x)\) = **evidence/reliability**
* \(C(x)\) = **causal usefulness**
* \(F(x)\) = **future relevance**
* \(U(x)\) = **uncertainty/noise/contradiction**
* \(\lambda\) = penalty for uncertainty
* \(\theta\) = minimum threshold for selecting the data
* \(\mathbb 1[\cdot]\) = 1 if the threshold is reached, otherwise 0.

Then your selected data is

$$
\boxed{
X_{\rm destined}
=
\operatorname*{arg\,top}_{x\in X}D(x)
}
$$

and the transformation step becomes

$$
\boxed{
S_{t+1}
=
\mathcal T
\left(
X_{\rm destined},
S_t,
O_t
\right)
}
$$

So the whole pipeline becomes:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Raw data}
\rightarrow
\text{Score}
\rightarrow
\text{Filter}
\rightarrow
\text{Extract}
\rightarrow
\text{Transform}
\rightarrow
\text{Realized state}
}
$$

### The important addition: richness

I would define richness itself as multidimensional:

$$
\boxed{
R(x)=
w_HH+
w_CC+
w_KK+
w_NN+
w_DD+
w_AA
}
$$

with:

* \(H\): information quantity
* \(C\): structural complexity
* \(K\): knowledge/compressibility
* \(N\): novelty
* \(D\): dependency/correlation
* \(A\): agency/causal influence

This prevents “large amount of data” from automatically meaning “valuable information.”

### Connecting it to Destined Entropy

Your earlier loop can therefore become:

$$
\boxed{
TE
\rightarrow NT
\rightarrow NR
\rightarrow P
\rightarrow
\color{black}{D(x)}
\rightarrow
DE
\rightarrow A^*
\rightarrow S'
}
$$

More formally:

$$
\boxed{
S_{t+1}
=
\mathcal T
\left[
\operatorname*{arg\,top}_{x\in X_t}
\left(
\frac{
R_xQ_xE_xC_xF_x
}{
1+\lambda U_x
}
\right),
S_t,O_t
\right]
}
$$

That is potentially the strongest single equation in your framework because it does **not claim that information automatically becomes reality**. It says:

> **Information becomes a candidate for realization when it has sufficient richness, quality, evidence, causal usefulness, and future relevance, while uncertainty/noise is sufficiently low.**

The final “turn real” operation is therefore not magic; it is the transformation operator \(\mathcal T\):

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal T:
\text{selected information}
\rightarrow
\text{physical/implemented state}
}
$$

For example:

$$
\text{neural pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{decoded representation}
\rightarrow
\text{memory/model}
\rightarrow
\text{machine implementation}
$$

or, in your more speculative extension:

$$
\text{neural information}
\rightarrow
\text{meta-state}
\rightarrow
\text{substrate-independent encoding}
\rightarrow
\text{reconstruction}.
$$

The last stages are **speculative engineering/philosophical hypotheses**, not currently demonstrated physics.

